Summary: Run Applications as Containers and Pods
1. Container
A container is an isolated process that includes everything required to run an application.
It contains:
•	application code
•	runtime environment
•	system libraries
•	dependencies
Example runtimes include Docker and Podman.
Key idea:
Container = application + runtime dependencies
This allows the application to run consistently on any system.
________________________________________
2. Pods
In Kubernetes, containers are grouped into pods.
A pod:
•	is the smallest deployable unit
•	contains one or more containers
•	represents one application
Containers inside a pod share:
•	network (same IP address)
•	storage volumes
•	Linux namespaces
Example structure:
Pod
 ├── Container 1
 └── Container 2
Usually:
1 pod = 1 container = 1 application
________________________________________
3. Container Images
A container image is a template used to create containers.
Image contents include:
•	application code
•	runtime environment
•	libraries
•	configuration
•	startup command
When an image runs, it creates a container instance.
Process:
Container Image → Container Instance → Running Application
________________________________________
4. Container Registries
Container images are stored in container registries.
Registries allow:
•	storing images
•	sharing images
•	distributing images to clusters
Common registries include:
•	Red Hat Container Catalog
•	Red Hat Quay (Quay.io)
Example image location:
registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel9/mysql-80
________________________________________
5. Inspecting Container Images
You can inspect container images using CLI tools.
Two common tools:
oc image
Used inside Red Hat OpenShift.
Example:
oc image info IMAGE_NAME
________________________________________
Skopeo
Skopeo inspects container images without downloading them.
Example:
skopeo inspect docker://registry/image
Information retrieved includes:
•	image digest
•	environment variables
•	exposed ports
•	commands
•	tags
________________________________________
6. Containers Are Immutable
Containers are designed to be immutable.
Immutable means:
The container should not be modified after creation.
If changes are required:
1.	Update the container image
2.	Build a new image
3.	Redeploy the container
________________________________________
7. Containers Are Ephemeral
Containers are also ephemeral.
Meaning:
Containers are temporary and can be destroyed and recreated at any time.
If a container fails:
Delete container
Recreate container from image
________________________________________
8. Container Troubleshooting Rule
Avoid modifying running containers.
Correct approach:
1 Detect issue
2 Fix container image
3 Rebuild image
4 Redeploy container
Only modify running containers for debugging purposes.
________________________________________
Key Concepts (Exam Quick Review)
Concept	Meaning
Container	Isolated application runtime
Pod	Smallest Kubernetes deployment unit
Container Image	Template used to create containers
Registry	Storage for container images
Immutable	Container should not change
Ephemeral	Containers are temporary
________________________________________
One-Sentence Summary
OpenShift runs applications as containers inside pods, using container images stored in registries, and containers are designed to be immutable and ephemeral.

